ema rule: 'antipattern_id takes the code-review-tests.md name where one applies, else one of the three ids above' (plan Approach / discovery.md:194)","code-review-tests.md:56 renders the class as bold prose '**fail-open oracle**', not a hyphenated id token — there is no canonical machine value to copy","references/patterns.md:137 defines the portable id as 'fail-open-oracle' (hyphenated)","Plan Approach lists the fallback vocabulary as 'the three ids below plus fail-open-oracle', confirming the portable id form","antipattern_id is emitted by a discovery subagent into the Phase-2 pool (discovery.md:203 'pooled, deduped list'), where value spelling matters for dedup/grouping"],"finding":"The antipattern_id enum value for the fail-open class is not canonicalized across the two sources a subagent may read: code-review-tests.md names it as the bolded prose phrase 'fail-open oracle' (:56) and supplies no id token, while references/patterns.md defines the id 'fail-open-oracle' (:137). Because antipattern_id is an LLM-emitted structured-output field, the same finding will be tagged 'fail-open oracle' in a rebar checkout (deriving the value from the rubric prose) but 'fail-open-oracle' on a portable repo, so any downstream consumer keying on the value sees two spellings for one class.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f405341e531821ac4","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"phases/discovery.md:194-197 (antipattern_id schema) with references/patterns.md:137 and src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md:56","norm_id":"ne77893720366c246","priority":0.5956,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A rebar-checkout subagent tags a fail-open finding 'fail-open oracle'; a portable-repo subagent tags the identical class 'fail-open-oracle'; a pooling/dedup or scoring step treats them as distinct antipattern classes","An operator greps the finding pool for a canonical id and misses half the fail-open findings"],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"I